        "heading": "Worked triage: name the category, ask the first question",
        "content": "Use the method from the earlier sections on three short alerts. For each: name the likely category, give the evidence, then pick the first question. Cover the answers and try before reading on.\n\n### Alert 1\n\n```\n09:41 WAF  rule=RateLimit  host=shop.example.com  requests=96,000 in 60s\n09:41 WAF  distinct source IPs=41,300  user-agent=(blank or 3 values)\n09:42 App  p95 response time 11.8s (normal 0.4s)\n```\n\n**Category:** Application-layer DDoS (a family of DDoS aimed at the web application itself). Evidence: huge request volume, thousands of sources, few distinct user agents, and slow responses. The CIA leg at stake is availability.\n\n**First question:** Is the traffic real customers or bots? Check whether requests hit one costly URL, whether sources are residential IPs or cloud ranges, and whether the CDN or anti-DDoS provider can apply a challenge or rate limit. Compare with the marketing calendar before declaring an attack.\n\n### Alert 2\n\n```\n11:20 EDR  host=hr-pc-07  proc=AcmeUpdater.exe (signed by Acme Corp, valid)\n11:20 EDR  AcmeUpdater.exe loaded unsigned module version.dll from the install folder\n11:21 NET  hr-pc-07 -> 198.51.100.200:443  first-seen domain (4 days old)\n```\n\n**Category:** Possible supply-chain compromise, perhaps a trojanized update. Evidence: a trusted, validly signed program behaves in a new way, loads an unsigned DLL and calls a brand-new domain. The signature passing does not clear it, as in SUNBURST.\n\n**First question:** Did the behavior start right after an update, and how many other hosts run the same version and show the same behavior? Check the vendor advisory and scope fleet-wide before closing it as a false positive.\n\n### Alert 3\n\n```\n16:05 SW   arpwatch flip flop 10.8.0.1 aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:01 (aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:99)\n16:06 PROXY 14 users, same floor: TLS certificate warning for mail.example.com\n```\n\n**Category:** Man-in-the-middle through ARP spoofing. Evidence: the gateway address changes MAC, and many users on one network segment see certificate errors at once.\n\n**First question:** Which switch port and device owns the second MAC address? Isolate that port while a network engineer confirms the real router's MAC, then tell users not to enter credentials until the investigation ends.\n\n### Habits to take away\n\n- Name a category, but state the evidence behind it.\n- Ask for scope: how many hosts, users or customers show the same pattern?\n- Ask for time: when did it start, and what changed just before?\n- Escalate when the pattern suggests a patient actor or a trusted supplier.\n- Record your hypothesis and update it when new data arrives."
